  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A more compact and insightful way to express allele-frequency change was presented by Sewall Wright, one of the founding fathers (with Fisher and Haldane) of modern selection theory. Because $$ \\begin{aligned}\\frac{d\\overline{W}}{dp}&=\\frac{d\\left(p^{2}W_{AA}+2p(1-p)W_{Aa}+(1-p)^{2}W_{aa}\\right)}{dp}\\\\&=2pW_{AA}+2(1-2p)W_{Aa}-2(1-p)W_{aa}\\end{aligned} $$ a little algebra shows that Equation 5.1b can be written as $$ \\Delta p=\\frac{p(1-p)}{2\\overline{W}}\\frac{d\\overline{W}}{dp}=\\frac{p(1-p)}{2}\\frac{d\\ln\\overline{W}}{dp} $$",
      "source_page": 116,
      "source_pages": [
        116
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_chapter5:p007:b5",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p007:b6",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p007:b7",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p007:b9",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p007:b10"
      ],
